    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What the spec required\nA new owner's `membershipLevel` must be capped at **one above the highest `membershipLevel` among their existing household members**; with no household member, no cap applies.\n\nA prerequisite surfaced while making the acceptance test green: the app previously rejected a second same-household owner with **409** unless the caller passed `sharesHousehold=true` (I confirmed this empirically \u2014 `b\u2192409`, `c` with the flag `\u2192201`). For a household to *have* members to cap against, that member must be creatable. So this checkpoint replaces \"reject the household duplicate\" with \"accept the member, but cap their level.\"\n\n## Changes (kept small and cohesive)\n- **`Households.java`** \u2014 added `ceilingLevel(existing, candidate)` returning *(max member level) + 1*, or `null` when there's no housemate, plus a tiny private `effectiveLevel` helper that respects each member's own cap. This puts the new logic in a small, low-complexity class rather than growing a large one.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 one line to set the cap (`owner.setMembershipLevelCap(Households.ceilingLevel(owners, owner))`) and removed the 3-line household-duplicate `409` gate.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 a persistent `membershipLevelCap` field + accessors, so the cap survives to `GET` (the test verifies via a re-fetch).\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `membershipLevel` mapping now returns `min(levelOf, cap)` when a cap is set, else the computed level.\n- **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql` / `data.sql`** \u2014 new `membership_level_cap` column and a matching `NULL` in the positional seed inserts (test profile is `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`; the other dialects don't track these evolving columns, so I left them as-is).\n\nBoth the new `coreCapsLevelByHousehold` test and the full suite are green.\n\nOne thing to flag for your judgment: removing the household-duplicate 409 gate is a real behavior change beyond the literal \"cap\" sentence, but it's forced by the test (which creates two same-household owners without `sharesHousehold` and expects both to succeed). `Households.isDuplicate` is now unused \u2014 I left it in place rather than churn the file further, but it can be deleted if you'd prefer no dead code.",
